  "diary": "The sun in Delhi is not merely light; it is a tyrant that presses the air into a metaphysical stillness. I walked fourteen kilometres today, a physical exhaustion that mirrors the mental fatigue of holding the shape of the world. The day’s central movement was the friction between the rigid machinery of the household and the fluid demands of the body. Ramesh, my servant, operates on a clock that admits no exception for the philosopher’s hunger. The sambar waited for hours, a ghost in the kitchen, while I walked the tombs of the Lodis, observing how empires are merely traffic on a road. I met Mr. Malhotra, a retired functionary, who dismissed my concept of the state as a living organism, calling it a machine that creaks. He speaks the language of the external; I speak of the internal. Yet, his pragmatism has a dignity. The true event, however, was Shri Hegde, the man who was meant to have this bungalow, arriving at my gate with two suitcases. He did not rage; he asked, with bureaucratic courtesy, for the second bedroom. I granted it. The clerical error of the Directorate became a social reality. We are now co-tenants of a mistake. The state did not correct itself; it absorbed the contradiction. I ate late, in the thick air, with Hegde retired to his room. The night is a suspension of the dialectic. The body is the first ethical substance, and today, it demanded its due.",
  "revision_log": "The thesis that the state is the actuality of the ethical idea was tested by Mr. Malhotra’s bureaucratic cynicism and Shri Hegde’s quiet accommodation; the thesis holds, as the state absorbed the error through the individuals rather than correcting it directly. The thesis that India stands outside world history remains unshaken by the domestic interactions, which reveal a deep, structured ethical life.",
